00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:25.870
But anyway, to understand how Instagram became
so lucrative, we have to take a look back

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:26.870
at it’s roots which take us back 2009 to
a young man named Kevin Systrom.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.570
Kevin was your classic high achieving tech
bro.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.240
The man started off by attending Stanford
where he majored in management science and

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.060
engineering before turning around and interning
at Odeo which is what would eventually turn

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.260
into Twitter before finally getting a job
at Google.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:46.800
So, Kevin was truly an elite bro, so I don’t
think you’d be surprised to hear that the

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.680
idea for instagram actually came rather naturally
during a vacation.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.790
It turns out that his girlfriend didn’t
want to post vacation pictures on social media

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.849
because she felt that the pictures weren’t
high enough quality.

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:02.370
Kevin’s solution to this was to just throw
on a few filters and this is what would lead

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:06.420
to the idea of Instagram, though it was originally
called Burbn.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.540
From there, it didn’t take long for Kevin
to put together a prototype for Burbn and

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.910
pitch it to a few VCs at a local party.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:18.140
They would agree to fund him with $500,000
allowing him to quit his job and go all in

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:19.390
on Burbn.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.330
Initially though, it seems that Kevin was
actually all over the place.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.700
He wanted the app to do as much as possible
from checking into hotels to earning points

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.300
for attending social events.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.480
It wasn’t until one of his college friends,
Mike Krieger, joined that company that their

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.239
vision became a lot more focused.

00:03:35.239 --> 00:03:40.299
Mike was adamant that people did not want
a complicated app and that simplicity was

00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:41.299
key.

00:03:41.299 --> 00:03:45.430
And as such, the duo would decide to hone
in on just photo sharing and make that as

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.260
polished as possible.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.389
And eventually on October 6, 2010, Instagram
would be launched on the app store.

00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:57.610
Within the first day itself, Instagram would
rack up 25,000 users.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:01.140
By the end of the first week, this turned
into 100,000 users.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.260
And by mid december, this turned into 1 million
users.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.409
One of the main reasons for this explosive
growth was Instagram’s crystal clear focus

00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:13.980
which translated into a crystal clear demographic
which is the first reason that Instagram is

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.170
so lucrative.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:20.100
Their demographic is not only crystal clear,
it’s also highly monetizable.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.580
Just think about it, what type of person is
most likely to use Instagram?

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:29.229
With YouTube, it’s basically everyone as
YouTube serves all sorts of content from comedy

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:32.039
and gaming to education and finance.

00:04:32.039 --> 00:04:36.990
At first glance, it seems that Instagram also
serves all sort of content but when we take

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:43.070
a step back and look at the overarching picture,
one adjective comes to mind: aspirational.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.009
More times than not, people don’t go onto
instagram to look at something truly funny

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:50.330
or entertaining or eduative like they might
with YouTube.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:54.180
They go on Instagram to look at things that
they don’t have whether it’s the perfect

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.570
body or the perfect relationship or the perfect
vacation or the perfect wealth.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:02.870
And the fact that people only post highly
doctored curated versions of themselves only

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.639
plays into this phenomena.

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:08.880
This is why Instagram is commonly cited as
the worst social media for mental health.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.509
But, while that sucks for users of the platform,
it’s perfect for the platform itself as

00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:17.620
this creates the perfect breeding ground for
advertisers to play into users’ insecurities

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.550
and dreams and maximize on conversation rates.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:26.360
When you combine this with the resources and
influence of Facebook, well, all of this gets

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.590
put into overdrive.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:37.930
THE FACEBOOK EFFECT:
Facebook got involved with Instagram pretty

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.229
early on, just 18 months after launch in fact
in April of 2012.

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:47.759
Facebook would buy out the entire company
for just $1 billion which at the time seemed

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:51.880
like a massive amount, but Zuckerberg clearly
knew what he was doing.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:57.039
After the acquisition, Kevin was for the most
part allowed to run Instagram as an independent

00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:02.940
business that simply had access to the resources
of Facebook which was extremely powerful.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:07.419
Instagram had the agility and speed of a small
startup but also the resources and backing

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:09.030
of a social media giant.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:14.230
And Kevin would make good use of this by venturing
into the world of copying.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:17.110
There’s really no easy way to put this.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:21.320
With the help of Facebook, Instagram would
just go around and blatantly rip off features

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:22.740
from the competition.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:28.520
The most blatant examples of this are of course
stories, IGTV, and Reels but there are dozens

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.030
of smaller examples.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.570
Usually, there’s no point in suing when
it comes to tech because even if you somehow

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.729
win the court case, the infringer will probably
come out on top anyway.

00:06:38.729 --> 00:06:43.930
If Instagram was a startup, maybe the competition
would have atleast tried, but since they were

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.580
now part of Facebook, no one even bothered
giving Instagram free reign to experiment

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.180
with whatever feature they wanted.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:54.530
Instagram was never adamant about any one
of theses features though.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.530
They always had the approach of let’s see
how users respond and how this affects the

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.180
business overall and then make a decision.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.310
Pulling back on Reels is just the latest example
of this.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:09.430
Just two years ago, they completely scrapped
the idea of long form content through IGTV

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.370
because according to themselves, users just
weren’t using it.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:18.370
This freedom to experiment with anything and
everything with little repercussions and consequences

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:23.310
gave Instagram the opportunity to really find
the most successful and lucrative features

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.150
and double down on them.

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:32.020
But, that only tells half the story as Facebook
brought something even more important to Instagram:

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.430
advertisers.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:37.430
Before Facebook got involved, Instagram didn’t
even have advertising.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:43.340
In fact, it’s not clear if Instagram made
any revenue at all, but Facebook would change

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:44.340
this.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:49.139
In November of 2013, Instagram would show
off their first sponsored post: an ad from

00:07:49.139 --> 00:07:50.740
Michael Kors.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:54.470
Users weren’t too happy with this ad but
Michael Kors was.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:08:00.930
In fact, within just the first 18 hours, Michael
Kors would gain 34,000 new followers.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:05.250
Instagram ads are obviously nowhere near as
effective today as users have gotten used

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:10.210
to them but compared to other platforms, Instagram
is still miles ahead.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:15.870
The average cost per ad click on Instagram
comes in at $3.56 which is even higher than

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:19.020
YouTube which comes in at $3.21.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:23.740
This is especially surprising given that on
YouTube, ads usually come in the format of

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.300
unskippable videos.

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:29.370
On Instagram, you can easily scroll past any
ad.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.640
So, you would think that getting clicks on
YouTube would be more expensive.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.029
But, it’s actually more expensive on Instagram
largely because of their demographics and

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:39.029
context.

00:08:39.029 --> 00:08:42.460
It’s easier to sell a product to someone
who wants to be something that they’re not

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:46.420
as opposed to someone who just wants to get
to the video already.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:51.100
The only social network that has a higher
cost per click that Instagram is LinkedIn

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.110
but that’s a wholenother beast.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:57.380
Between the freedom to experiment and opportunity
to leverage first in class advertising, it

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:02.839
seemed like Kevin’s partnership with Zuckerberg
was going quite well but this honeymoon phase

00:09:02.839 --> 00:09:05.410
didn’t last forever.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:14.130
NO NEED TO SHARE:
As Instagram started doing better and better

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:19.200
and even started outshining Facebook itself,
the company started having some serious internal

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.200
politics.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.399
The Instagram team felt that Facebook got
a steal with the acquisiton and that they

00:09:24.399 --> 00:09:26.180
were the future of Facebook.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:31.050
Meanwhile, the Facebook team felt that that
only reason that Instagram was so successful

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:33.120
was because of Facebook’s backing.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.130
And as you would guess, this drama went all
the way to the top to Mark Zuckerberg and

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.330
Kevin Systrom.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:43.930
Eventually, it got to the point that Zuckerberg
just wanted Kevin and Mike to leave so that

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:48.210
he could take the reigns of both Facebook
and Instagram and reunite the companies.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:52.400
Zuckerberg didn’t want to flat out fire
these guys though, so instead, he would just

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:56.110
start cutting resources and implementing changes
that would piss them off.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:00.820
For example, when Kevin was on paternity leave,
Zuckerberg would implement location tracking

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:05.790
and hamburger menus into the app, two features
that Kevin was strongly against.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:11.740
Eventually, Zuckerberg would come out on top
and both Kevin and Mike would leave, But,

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:16.500
being the undisputed king of both Instagram
and Facebook wasn’t the only place that

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.769
Zuckerberg didn’t have to share.

00:10:18.769 --> 00:10:22.519
One major advantage that we have yet to discuss
is that Instagram doesn’t have to share

00:10:22.519 --> 00:10:27.060
any of their revenue with their creators largely
because their creators aren’t after cash

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:28.120
directly.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:32.100
More times than not, the main goal of Instagram
users whether it’s the popular kid in high

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:37.390
school or some massive celebrity is to boost
popularity and stay relevant as opposed to

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:38.510
making money.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:44.329
Of course, money is part of it but with Instagram,
it’s more of something that follows as opposed

00:10:44.329 --> 00:10:46.020
to something that leads.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:49.950
As such, the fact that Instagram doesn’t
share revenue with their creators isn’t

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:52.290
something that really turns away creators.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:56.960
Of course, there are some exceptions to this
and Instagram does pay creators under certain

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.540
circumstances, but it comes to main feed,
Instagram gets to keep all the revenue which

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:05.320
obviously makes a massive difference.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:10.130
YouTube shares basically half of all their
revenue with creators meaning that the total

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:14.760
revenue that YouTube pulls in is not $30 billion
but actually closer to $60 billion or higher

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.310
than Instagram as you would expect.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:19.640
So, why is Instagram so lucrative?

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:23.980
Well, they have a unique aspirational demographic
that’s perfect for advertising.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:28.470
They’ve been able to experiment with every
format under the sun to find the most lucrative

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.000
and successful formats.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.210
And, historically, they haven’t had to share
much revenue with creators.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:39.790
When you combine all of this, you end up with
over $50 billion in annual revenue.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:45.080
Meta as a whole pulls in $126 billion in annual
revenue and they’re worth just over $800

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:46.080
billion.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.190
So, if we were to translate this ratio over
to Instagram, it would mean that Instagram

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:54.250
is worth over $300 billion by themselves.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:58.459
And that’s why Instagram is the real winner
when it comes to short form content.

00:11:58.459 --> 00:12:04.639
They’re not staying out of the loop by ditching
Reels altogether, but they’re also not willing

00:12:04.639 --> 00:12:08.700
to cannablize their main business in order
to make Reels work.

